WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.370
- [Tutor] Let g of x equal one over x.

00:02.370 --> 00:03.608
Can we use the mean value theorem

00:03.608 --> 00:06.950
to say that the equation g
prime of x is equal to one half

00:06.950 --> 00:08.580
has a solution where negative one

00:08.580 --> 00:10.170
is less than x is less than two,

00:10.170 --> 00:11.805
if so, write a justification.

00:11.805 --> 00:13.260
Alright, pause this video

00:13.260 --> 00:15.510
and see if you can figure that out.

00:15.510 --> 00:17.750
So the key to using
the mean value theorem,

00:17.750 --> 00:19.810
even before you even think about using it,

00:19.810 --> 00:20.770
you have to make sure

00:20.770 --> 00:23.730
that you are continuous
over the closed interval

00:23.730 --> 00:25.730
and differentiable over the open interval,

00:25.730 --> 00:26.955
so this is the open interval here

00:26.955 --> 00:30.540
and then the closed interval
would include the end points.

00:30.540 --> 00:32.370
But you might immediately realize

00:32.370 --> 00:35.210
that both of these intervals
contain x equals zero

00:35.210 --> 00:38.570
and if x equals zero,
the function is undefined

00:38.570 --> 00:39.682
and if it's undefined there,

00:39.682 --> 00:41.440
well it's not going to continuous

00:41.440 --> 00:43.090
or differentiable at that point

00:43.090 --> 00:44.593
and so no,

00:45.540 --> 00:48.240
not continuous

00:48.240 --> 00:50.920
or differentiable,

00:50.920 --> 00:52.000
differentiable

00:53.170 --> 00:56.250
over the interval,

00:56.250 --> 00:57.318
over the interval.

00:57.318 --> 00:59.170
Alright, let's do the second part.

00:59.170 --> 01:00.330
Can we use the mean value theorem

01:00.330 --> 01:03.010
to say that there is a value
c such that g prime of c

01:03.010 --> 01:04.530
is equal to negative one half

01:04.530 --> 01:06.660
and one is less than c is less than two

01:06.660 --> 01:08.390
if so, write a justification.

01:08.390 --> 01:10.490
So pause the video again.

01:10.490 --> 01:13.100
Alright, so in this
situation, between one and two

01:13.100 --> 01:14.836
on both the open and the closed intervals,

01:14.836 --> 01:16.580
well, this is a rational function

01:16.580 --> 01:18.360
and a rational function
is going to be continuous

01:18.360 --> 01:20.900
and differentiable at
every point in its domain

01:20.900 --> 01:22.980
and its domain completely contains

01:22.980 --> 01:24.847
this open and closed interval

01:24.847 --> 01:26.260
or another way to think about it,

01:26.260 --> 01:28.140
every point on this open interval

01:28.140 --> 01:31.330
and on the closed
interval is in the domain,

01:31.330 --> 01:33.800
so we can write g of x

01:33.800 --> 01:36.180
is a rational

01:37.100 --> 01:37.933
function,

01:40.110 --> 01:43.420
which lets us know that it is continuous

01:43.420 --> 01:48.420
and differentiable at
every point in its domain,

01:48.760 --> 01:51.850
at every point

01:53.560 --> 01:55.963
in its domain,

01:57.460 --> 01:59.830
the closed interval from one to two

01:59.830 --> 02:02.580
is in domain

02:03.623 --> 02:06.560
and so now, let's see what
the average rate of change is

02:06.560 --> 02:08.020
from one to two

02:08.020 --> 02:10.680
and so we get g of two

02:10.680 --> 02:12.380
minus g of one

02:12.380 --> 02:15.420
over two minus one is equal to one half

02:15.420 --> 02:20.110
minus one over one, which is
equal to negative one half,

02:20.110 --> 02:21.510
therefore,

02:21.510 --> 02:23.320
therefore

02:23.320 --> 02:25.540
by the

02:25.540 --> 02:27.930
mean value theorem,

02:27.930 --> 02:32.537
there must be a c

02:33.440 --> 02:37.540
where one is less than c is less than two

02:37.540 --> 02:42.540
and g prime of c is equal to

02:42.750 --> 02:46.390
the average rate of change
between the end points,

02:46.390 --> 02:48.637
negative one half

02:48.637 --> 02:51.463
and we're done, so we can put
a big yes right over there

02:51.463 --> 02:53.283
and then this is our justification.
